Ladybird includes empty inline boxes in the alignment of lines with
text, atomic content, or a forced break. Phantom lines retain zero
height and allow block margins to collapse. Each forced break adds
the metrics of its enclosing inline box, without letting the break
element’s own line-height enlarge the line.

Blink’s InlineBoxState::ComputeTextMetrics calculates inline text
metrics. InlineLayoutStateStack::AddBoxFragmentPlaceholder skips
vertical metrics when is_empty_line_ is set.

Gecko’s nsLineLayout::VerticalAlignLine calls SetSpanForEmptyLine
and sets zero block size when mLineIsEmpty is set. Occupied lines use
VerticalAlignFrames to include their inline boxes’ metrics.

WebKit’s LineBoxBuilder::constructInlineLevelBoxes marks the parent
inline box as contentful for preserved newlines and, in standards
mode, hard line breaks. Ladybird follows the same separation between
inline participation and phantom-line elision.

Chrome gives 56px to an inline with line-height:56px containing a
break, and 112px to two such breaks. An empty inline followed by
two breaks gives 75px: Only the first line includes that inline.
A solitary empty inline stays 0px. The imported empty-inline and
phantom-line WPT cases exercise the same distinction.

Empty inline boxes remain pending through line fitting, so an oversized
first word or image stays on its first occupied line. A whitespace-only
inline before a soft wrap contributes its metrics to the preceding
line. Chrome gives the same results.
